<!-- p1-public-quality:start --><section class="section wrap p1-public-quality" data-p1-public-quality="public-consequence-contracts.html" data-reviewed="2026-08-04"><div class="eyebrow">Accountability tracking · reviewed 2026-08-04</div><h2>Record the promise, the deadline and the real-world outcome.</h2><div class="grid"><article class="card"><h3>What this is</h3><p>The Accountability Twin converts a public statement, commitment, investigation or policy action into a dated record that can be checked against what happened later.</p></article><article class="card"><h3>Strongest record</h3><p>A strong contract contains the original source, named actor, exact wording or action, date, measurable consequence and a future review point.</p></article><article class="card"><h3>Why it matters</h3><p>Tracking outcomes reduces the advantage of short news cycles. It allows readers to distinguish a promise from delivery, an announcement from implementation and an allegation from a verified consequence.</p></article><article class="card"><h3>What it does not prove</h3><p>The tracker does not predict guilt or automatically decide whether a promise failed. Context, changed conditions, appeals, corrections and partial outcomes must be recorded before a verdict is made.</p></article><article class="card"><h3>What to do next</h3><p>Follow a contract, inspect its evidence record and return at the review date to see whether the promised consequence occurred.</p></article></div><div class="cta-row"><a class="btn alt" href="evidence-vault.html">Inspect the Evidence Vault</a><a class="btn alt" href="live-intel.html">See what changed</a><a class="btn alt" href="books.html">Open the book library</a><a class="btn alt" href="videos.html">Watch the video archive</a><a class="btn alt" href="newsletter.html">Receive the free briefing</a><a class="btn alt" href="corrections.html">Corrections and challenges</a><a class="btn alt" href="optin-center.html">Choose a free briefing</a></div><p class="evidence-note"><strong>Correction route:</strong> challenge a name, date, figure, source classification or conclusion through <a href="corrections.html">Corrections and Challenges</a>. The public review date records this page-level editorial pass; source publication and event dates remain attached to individual records.</p></section><!-- p1-public-quality:end -->
